  "prose_summary": "Section 3.1 reasons through what numbers can communicate beyond simple counting. Students encounter a row of children of varying heights, each announcing a number representing how many of their immediate neighbours are taller. Through a Math Talk prompt and seven questions, students justify whether particular sequences of 0s, 1s, and 2s are achievable — for instance, why a child at the end cannot say '2' (they have only one neighbour), or why the sequence 1,1,1,1,1 is impossible in a group of five children of different heights. The reasoning is structural: students must think about constraints imposed by position and relative height, making this section a reasoning exercise in combinatorial logic rather than arithmetic.\n\nSection 3.2 defines the concept of a 'supercell' — a cell in a row or grid whose number is strictly greater than all adjacent cells — and then reasons through its properties before applying the definition. The definition is introduced via a worked table where students are asked to notice and explain why certain cells are coloured, and then extends to two-dimensional grids where adjacency includes top, bottom, left, and right neighbours. The Figure it Out block (10 questions) moves from marking supercells in a given row, to filling tables so that supercells fall exactly on pre-coloured cells, to exploring the maximum number of supercells possible and whether a table can be filled with no supercells at all. The reasoning act (justifying why the largest number is always a supercell; why the smallest can never be one) clearly precedes the application act (constructing tables to specification), making the dual goal sequence evident.\n\nSection 3.3 applies number line skills to place large numbers accurately in position. The section opens by placing eleven numbers (2180, 2754, 1500, 3600, 9950, 9590, 1050, 3050, 5030, 5300, 8400) on a number line spanning 1000 to 10,000. The Figure it Out block presents four number lines with different scales — units of 5, units of 1, units of 1, and units of 1000 — where students identify unlabelled positions and label remaining ones, then circle the smallest and box the largest. The section also includes a 5-digit number completion table using the digits 1, 0, 6, 3, 9 where only coloured cells may hold a number greater than all its neighbours, consolidating supercell logic within a multi-row grid at a larger number scale.\n\nSection 3.4 reasons about digit sums and digit frequency. It opens by establishing the count of numbers with 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 digits (9, 90, 900, 9000, 90,000) through a table-completion task. Komal's observation that different numbers can share the same digit sum (68, 176, 545 all sum to 14) launches a Figure it Out block: students write numbers with digit sum 14, find the smallest and largest such numbers within a 5-digit constraint, and explore how large a number with digit sum 14 can be. The 'Digit Detectives' interlude asks students to reason about how many times the digit '7' appears in counting from 1 to 100 and from 1 to 1000, requiring systematic thinking about tens-place and units-place occurrences — a reasoning task about structure rather than a calculation.\n\nSection 3.5 reasons about palindromic numbers and the reverse-and-add procedure. After identifying the pattern in 66, 848, 575, 797, 1111, students write all 3-digit palindromes using the digits 1, 2, 3. The section then introduces the reverse-and-add process (add a number to its digit-reversal; if not a palindrome, repeat), illustrated through three examples that reach palindromes in one or two steps. A Math Talk prompt asks students to explore whether starting from any 2-digit number always yields a palindrome — the answer is yes for 2-digit numbers, though the note flags that 196 is suspected to never yield a palindrome. A puzzle-time challenge asks students to identify a 5-digit palindrome satisfying two digit-ratio constraints (h digit = double t digit; t digit = double u digit), which requires simultaneous reasoning about place value and palindrome symmetry.\n\nSection 3.6 applies a specific multi-step procedure — the Kaprekar process — to 4-digit numbers. Students take any 4-digit number with at least two distinct digits, form the largest and smallest rearrangements (call them A and B), compute C = A − B, and repeat using C's digits. The section walks through two complete iterations starting from 6382 before leaving two blanks for students to continue, demonstrating convergence to 6174. An Explore prompt asks students to verify this with different 4-digit numbers and then apply the same procedure to 3-digit numbers to discover that 495 plays the analogous role. The section is primarily an application: students execute the algorithm on chosen numbers and observe the invariant outcome.\n\nSection 3.7 applies number patterns and the concepts of palindromes and digit rearrangements to clock and calendar contexts. Students list all valid palindromic times on a 12-hour clock (e.g., 4:44, 10:01, 12:21) and all dates where digits read the same forwards and backwards. A calendar puzzle asks whether any year's calendar can repeat — the answer, given in the solution key, depends on the positioning of leap years within the cycle. The Figure it Out block (four questions) integrates skills from the chapter: choosing 4-digit sets to maximise or minimise digit-rearrangement differences and sums, computing palindrome sums and differences, finding the next palindromic time after 10:01, and determining how many Kaprekar rounds 5683 requires to reach 6174 (answer: 8 rounds).\n\nSection 3.8 applies mental arithmetic strategies to additions and subtractions involving large 5-digit numbers. The first activity presents a spider-web figure where numbers in a central column (400, 1500, 13000, 25000, 28000, 61600) must be combined — using each as many times as needed, with addition only — to produce target sums on the sides. Students think about which multiples and combinations of the given numbers reach each target. A second variant permits both addition and subtraction, and a third Figure it Out block asks students to write examples of multi-digit additions and subtractions satisfying given digit-count constraints (e.g., 5-digit + 3-digit giving a 6-digit sum) and to classify statements about digit-count outcomes as always, sometimes, or never true. This section is almost entirely applied computation and mental strategy.\n\nSection 3.9 reasons about and applies efficient counting strategies for visually arranged arrays of numbers. Six figures present rectangular or diamond grids of repeated numbers — 40s, 50s, 32s, 64s, mixed 15/25/35s, and powers of 2 — and students are asked to find the sum. The key pedagogical point is to reason about whether one-by-one addition is necessary, or whether a quicker method (counting the number of each value and multiplying) can be applied. The Math Talk prompt emphasises sharing different solution strategies, making the reasoning about computational efficiency as important as the sum itself. This dual arc — reasoning about why a shortcut works, then applying it — is clearly sequential in the section's structure.\n\nSection 3.10 reasons about the Collatz conjecture. The rule (halve if even; multiply by 3 and add 1 if odd; repeat) is illustrated through four sequences that all terminate at 1. Students are invited to generate their own Collatz sequences, evaluate whether they always reach 1, and reflect on whether Collatz's 1937 conjecture is likely to be true. The section explicitly frames this as an unsolved problem in mathematics, making the pedagogical purpose one of mathematical reasoning and conjecture rather than algorithmic computation. No formula is derived; the dominant act is reasoning about pattern and justification.\n\nSection 3.11 applies estimation to real-world magnitudes. After motivating why estimates are often sufficient, the section works through Paromita's estimate of her school population (32 + 29 + 35 ≈ 100 per class × 5 classes ≈ 500). The Figure it Out block asks students to estimate step counts across various distances, blink/breath rates across various time intervals, and names objects numbering in the thousands or tens of thousands. A second sub-block asks quick yes/no estimates (number of words in the maths textbook; school bus riders; cost of fruit custard; Gandhinagar–Kohima distance; Sheetal's claimed 13,000 school hours). Students then make their own estimation challenges. The dominant act throughout is applying reasoning about scale and magnitude to arrive at justified approximate values.\n\nSection 3.12 reasons about game theory and winning strategies through number games. Game #1: players alternately add 1, 2, or 3 to a running total; first to reach 21 wins. Game #2: start between 1 and 10; alternately add 1–10; first to reach 99 wins. Students play multiple rounds and are asked to identify the winning pattern — which player can always win, and what sequence of numbers to target. The Figure it Out block consolidates skills from the entire chapter: a supercell grid manipulation (swap two digits to create four supercells from one); a Kaprekar round count for a birth year; identifying 5-digit odd-digit numbers closest to 50,000; estimation questions; digit-sum constructions; number pattern sums; Collatz verification for 100; and a winning strategy for a variant starting at 0 targeting 22.",
