  "prose_summary": "Section 10.1 defines negative numbers, the integer number line, and the operations of addition and subtraction through the rich, multi-layered context of Bela's Building of Fun — a multi-storied building with floors both above and below ground. The ground floor is Floor 0, floors above are positive, and floors below are negative. A lift with '+' (up) and '–' (down) buttons introduces the notation +n and –n for movements. Floor numbering extends from below-ground shops like the Toy Store (Floor –1), Video Games (Floor –2), and Cinema (Floor –3) to above-ground venues like the Food Court (Floor +1), Art Centre (+2), Books (+3), and Ice Cream shop (+4). Students encounter the rule Starting Floor + Movement = Target Floor for addition, then discover that combining button presses is itself addition, leading naturally to the concept of additive inverse (the 'Back to Zero' idea: pressing +3 and then –3 returns to Floor 0). Comparison of integers is introduced using floor position: Floor –4 is lower and therefore less than Floor –3, establishing the ordering of integers on a vertical line. The section then applies subtraction as 'finding the movement needed': Target Floor – Starting Floor = Movement needed, with three worked examples covering (–1)–(–2), (–1)–(+3), and (+2)–(–2). This is extended to larger numbers through a mine context where levels range from –200 to +180 m, reinforcing addition and subtraction with larger integers. An infinite lift metaphor introduces the full integer number line (…–4,–3,–2,–1,0,1,2,3,4,…), connects the lift to the horizontal number line, and reveals that subtracting a negative number equals adding the corresponding positive. The subsections 'Adding and subtracting larger numbers', 'Adding, subtracting, and comparing any numbers', 'Back to the number line', 'Using the unmarked number line', and 'Converting subtraction to addition and addition to subtraction' all belong to this section's unfolding arc. Students apply these ideas across numerous Figure it Out sets involving both small and large integers.\n\nSection 10.2 applies the token model — a physical/visual representation using positive (green) and negative (red) tokens — to perform addition and subtraction of integers. The section opens with the lift attendant collecting positive and negative tokens in his pocket; after an hour, the number of tokens determines his floor. A zero pair (one positive + one negative = 0) is introduced as the key cancellation tool for addition. Students add integers by laying out tokens, removing all zero pairs, and reading the remainder. Subtraction using tokens is then demonstrated: taking away tokens of the required sign from an existing pool, with the key trick of adding zero pairs when there are not enough tokens of the required type to take away. Three worked examples — (+5)–(+6), (–7)–(–5), and +4–(–6) — demonstrate the technique systematically. The token model provides a concrete, manipulative justification for why subtracting a negative is the same as adding a positive.\n\nSection 10.3 applies integers to three real-world contexts. The first is banking (credits and debits): starting with a balance of ₹100, students track credits as positive changes and debits as negative changes, including the possibility of a negative balance. Figure it Out problems invite students to compute bank balances with multiple credits and debits. The second context is geographical cross-sections, where height above sea level is positive and below sea level is negative, using a cross-section chart with seven labelled points (A through G). Students read heights, order points, and research real-world extremes (Mount Everest, Challenger Deep). The third context is temperature in degrees Celsius, where 0°C is the freezing point of water, temperatures above are positive, and temperatures below are negative. Students identify sub-zero places in India and match temperature readings to times of day for Leh, Ladakh in November. These real-world connections consolidate integer sense and comparison.\n\nSection 10.4 applies integers to two exploratory puzzle activities. The first is a 'hollow integer grid' — a 3×3 grid where the border cells (top row, bottom row, left column, right column) must all sum to the same 'border sum'. Two example grids are given (border sum 0 and border sum –3), and students complete three more grids with specified border sums (+4, –2, –4), exploring multiple solutions and investigating which grids admit more than one filling. The second activity is an 'amazing grid of numbers' — a 4×4 integer grid where students circle any number, strike out its row and column, circle another unstruck number, and repeat; the sum of the four circled numbers is always the same regardless of choices. Two further grids are provided for exploration. A final standalone Figure it Out set consolidates integer arithmetic and ordering: writing integers between pairs, summing to –8, exploring sums achievable with integer dice, solving a table of eight arithmetic expressions, finding historical years using the integer line (including BCE considerations), completing sequences, creating expressions with integer cards, and determining the sign of results of various addition/subtraction combinations. The section closes with an integer Snakes and Ladders game extending from –50 to +50.\n\nSection 10.5 recalls the historical origins of integers, noting that negative numbers and zero were first formalised in Asia. Chinese work (Nine Chapters on Mathematical Art, c. 1st–2nd century CE) used red and black rods analogous to the chapter's tokens. India's Kautilya (Arthaśhāstra, c. 300 BCE) recognised negative bank balances; the Bakśhālī manuscript (c. 300 CE) used a special symbol for negatives. Brahmagupta's Brāhma-sphuṭa-siddhānta (628 CE) gave the first complete, explicit rules for addition and subtraction of positive numbers, negative numbers, and zero — essentially forming the modern integer ring. His five rules for addition and five rules for subtraction are quoted in the section. European resistance to negative numbers persisted into the 18th century (Lazare Carnot), but integers are now foundational and paved the way for algebra.",
